Subject: [PATCH] mm/page_reporting: report the whole free list per pass

Upstream reports at most a sixteenth of each free list per pass, one
pass every two seconds, so a guest that frees 2 GiB on a 4 GiB VM gives
90 % of it back to vmuc's host only after 7-10 s. vmuc releases a range
in tens of microseconds (p99 0.25-2.5 ms), so the pacing protects
nothing here; with the whole list per pass, the first pass (two seconds
after the free) returns it all. The two-second delay that batches
reports is unchanged.

Evidence: docs/experiments/2026-10-13-reporting-pace.md and
docs/adr/0002-guest-kernel-reports-everything-per-pass.md.

--- a/mm/page_reporting.c
+++ b/mm/page_reporting.c
@@ -169,16 +169,16 @@
 	 * device for this list. By doing this we avoid processing any
 	 * given list for too long.
 	 *
-	 * The current value used allows us enough calls to process over a
-	 * sixteenth of the current list plus one additional call to handle
-	 * any pages that may have already been present from the previous
-	 * list processed. This should result in us reporting all pages on
-	 * an idle system in about 30 seconds.
+	 * vmuc: enough calls for the whole list plus one, so a pass reports
+	 * everything that is free when it runs. Upstream allows a sixteenth
+	 * per pass (about 30 seconds for an idle system), which leaves a
+	 * freed gigabyte with the guest for 10 seconds or more; vmuc's host
+	 * releases a range in tens of microseconds.
 	 *
 	 * The division here should be cheap since PAGE_REPORTING_CAPACITY
 	 * should always be a power of 2.
 	 */
-	budget = DIV_ROUND_UP(area->nr_free, PAGE_REPORTING_CAPACITY * 16);
+	budget = DIV_ROUND_UP(area->nr_free, PAGE_REPORTING_CAPACITY);
 
 	/* loop through free list adding unreported pages to sg list */
 	list_for_each_entry_safe(page, next, list, lru) {
